{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1254 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "1f5e373f477faa2468cbc41a9dc4b630ab08f5e1398ca715ea75abfc740c18e8" :edb/db-before-t 1253 :edb/db-after-t 1254 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534566 50 #inst "2026-10-08T01:12:13.758Z" 13194139534566 true] [17592186063913 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186063913 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13979325/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" 13194139534566 false] [17592186064163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295794 13194139534566 true] [17592186064163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295801 13194139534566 true] [17592186064163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295808 13194139534566 true] [17592186064163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295822 13194139534566 true] [17592186064163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295825 13194139534566 true] [17592186064164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295812 13194139534566 true] [17592186064164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295829 13194139534566 true] [17592186064164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295832 13194139534566 true] [17592186064165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295816 13194139534566 true] [17592186064165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295846 13194139534566 true] [17592186064166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295787 13194139534566 true] [17592186064166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295819 13194139534566 true] [17592186064166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295836 13194139534566 true] [17592186064166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186295853 13194139534566 true] [17592186064865 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534566 true] [17592186064865 1100 "Consider the function $f(x) = x^{3} + 2x + 1$. Given that $y = f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $(f^{-1})^{′}(1)$." 13194139534566 false] [17592186064865 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+2x+1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186064865 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2x+1 & =1 \\\\ x({x}^{2}+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=0.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+2x+1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\cdot 0+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186261590 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274991 13194139534566 false] [17592186261590 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295840 13194139534566 true] [17592186261590 1100 "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{'}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{'}(3).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{'}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |" 13194139534566 false] [17592186261590 1100 "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(3).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |" 13194139534566 true] [17592186261590 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(3))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=5$ and ${f}^{'}(5)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{'}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 false] [17592186261590 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=5$ and ${f}^{′}(5)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186279595 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295828 13194139534566 true] [17592186283013 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295835 13194139534566 true] [17592186295787 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295788 13194139534566 true] [17592186295787 1089 "q-110338" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295787 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534566 true] [17592186295787 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+10}{x+1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4).$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295787 1113 #uuid "c30c5f22-36f0-5965-9fed-d4b105565e9d" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295787 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(4),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(4).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(4)=x.$ For $x\\ne -1,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(4) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =4 \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x+1} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1) & =4\\cdot (x+1) \\\\ \\frac{x+10}{\\cancel{(x+1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(x+1)} & =4x+4 \\\\ x+10 & =4x+4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(4)=2.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+10}{x+1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{9}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{9}{{(2+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{(3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{9} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295789 13194139534566 true] [17592186295788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295790 13194139534566 true] [17592186295788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295791 13194139534566 true] [17592186295788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295792 13194139534566 true] [17592186295788 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295793 13194139534566 true] [17592186295788 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534566 true] [17592186295788 1041 "selection" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295788 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295793 13194139534566 true] [17592186295788 1110 #uuid "8a6d83d3-c1bc-5852-905d-98bc3e78e569" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295789 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295789 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295789 1111 #uuid "c556d14b-70e2-5ca2-a713-25a69f146954" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295790 1034 "1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295790 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295790 1111 #uuid "45bd1e36-3565-5059-9978-17a29caaedef" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295791 1034 "-4" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295791 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295791 1111 #uuid "e2d66724-67af-5fbb-b742-1db1094234b4" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295792 1034 "-\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295792 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295792 1111 #uuid "7e593f1f-62bc-5168-932e-59309def1365" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295793 1034 "-1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295793 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295793 1111 #uuid "2aedf132-d24d-5212-9f42-ff8047e1aa2c" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295794 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295795 13194139534566 true] [17592186295794 1089 "q-15709" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295794 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534566 true] [17592186295794 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/0d7c1b55cbc73a769f7ab0c0c8e634d54356eb36e77dc7ca0d06ff77d9367162.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(-1,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(2,-1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295794 1113 #uuid "ba779119-012f-5aba-bccf-539b483e38de" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295794 1145 "Since $B\\,(2,-1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295795 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295796 13194139534566 true] [17592186295795 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295797 13194139534566 true] [17592186295795 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295798 13194139534566 true] [17592186295795 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295799 13194139534566 true] [17592186295795 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295800 13194139534566 true] [17592186295795 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534566 true] [17592186295795 1041 "selection" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295795 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295796 13194139534566 true] [17592186295795 1110 #uuid "48dfbb50-946c-5981-abd7-1282378dd376" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295796 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295796 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295796 1111 #uuid "acd009f3-ae84-5bf6-9c44-e7fcdf8b3a66" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295797 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295797 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295797 1111 #uuid "13cb97fe-9b74-58cd-89b8-6a209d64a358" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295798 1034 "2" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295798 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295798 1111 #uuid "bcc94adc-ca4b-504d-8858-062876eb3fef" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295799 1034 "1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295799 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295799 1111 #uuid "62c45b8f-4bd1-5b10-9494-818e9cc1998a" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295800 1034 "-1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295800 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295800 1111 #uuid "069c5138-e7b7-5507-822e-4670dae7e2db" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295801 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295802 13194139534566 true] [17592186295801 1089 "q-18601" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295801 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534566 true] [17592186295801 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/7e881b5cb3613ff72264739b1cbe1214c1041a78d201cfc323476910e7ce8e3c.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(4,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295801 1113 #uuid "2e2a8e81-0143-5ac3-9218-1837a0d8d00a" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295801 1145 "Since $(2,4)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=4.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [1,5]$ is $1$, hence, ${f}^{′}(4)=1.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295803 13194139534566 true] [17592186295802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295804 13194139534566 true] [17592186295802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295805 13194139534566 true] [17592186295802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295806 13194139534566 true] [17592186295802 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295807 13194139534566 true] [17592186295802 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534566 true] [17592186295802 1041 "selection" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295802 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295805 13194139534566 true] [17592186295802 1110 #uuid "4c9b5459-d3ea-5931-864a-91d802f0272f" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295803 1034 "3" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295803 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295803 1111 #uuid "73bf5e7b-fa30-5a4d-a081-82a6905392ea" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295804 1034 "2" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295804 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295804 1111 #uuid "6edc8b38-4fb2-5970-b364-9f9c67e2e9dd" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295805 1034 "1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295805 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295805 1111 #uuid "995de621-ea4f-5be3-9bfa-c3bea3b38f81" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295806 1034 "-3" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295806 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295806 1111 #uuid "37916253-c11b-5258-9880-f77aade288fb" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295807 1034 "-1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295807 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295807 1111 #uuid "fd9afa23-7f21-5bfd-99fe-558a3d95a546" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295808 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295809 13194139534566 true] [17592186295808 1089 "q-312578" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295808 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534566 true] [17592186295808 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295808 1113 #uuid "77c43205-3a68-5865-9baf-3488c8c39bcb" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295808 1145 "Since $(0,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295810 13194139534566 true] [17592186295809 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295811 13194139534566 true] [17592186295809 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534566 true] [17592186295809 1041 "field-1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295809 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295810 13194139534566 true] [17592186295809 1110 #uuid "40350093-241a-55dd-ac9f-9a83f78e707e" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295810 1034 "2" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295810 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295810 1111 #uuid "1fe6726f-14a9-5251-a055-84ac34260be2" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295811 1034 "1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295811 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295811 1111 #uuid "0f65b70c-4513-5d7f-88de-bf7e421f1942" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295812 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295813 13194139534566 true] [17592186295812 1089 "q-312580" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295812 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534566 true] [17592186295812 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+{2}^{x}$ and the point $P(0,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(1,0)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." 13194139534566 true] [17592186295812 1113 #uuid "9315fbdb-8a10-5769-ba1d-a74c3c6c47b5" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295812 1145 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+{2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x+{2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295814 13194139534566 true] [17592186295813 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295815 13194139534566 true] [17592186295813 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534566 true] [17592186295813 1041 "field-1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295813 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295814 13194139534566 true] [17592186295813 1110 #uuid "50ba0f3b-3db3-5e26-bb8f-963cf1a8bd6b" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295814 1034 "\\frac{1}{\\ln(2)}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295814 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295814 1111 #uuid "3c252298-e973-55f9-b09e-7bb41ab18e55" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295815 1034 "0" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295815 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295815 1111 #uuid "99ad25f2-831a-51b2-bf5b-1d70cd523db3" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295816 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295817 13194139534566 true] [17592186295816 1089 "q-312583" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295816 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534566 true] [17592186295816 1100 "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-5$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295816 1113 #uuid "d1490c31-6761-5f3f-8b46-c37dbd062af7" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295816 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=3$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295817 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295818 13194139534566 true] [17592186295817 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534566 true] [17592186295817 1041 "field-1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295817 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295818 13194139534566 true] [17592186295817 1110 #uuid "5fd9d412-937f-5c3d-b8be-8ecf613b4921" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295818 1034 "-\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295818 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295818 1111 #uuid "c0464f1b-b508-5d46-aab4-a160247b57b0" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295819 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295820 13194139534566 true] [17592186295819 1089 "q-312585" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295819 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534566 true] [17592186295819 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\ge 1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(8)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295819 1113 #uuid "7cdbca75-994b-53ca-b31e-22e3ae38d84f" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295819 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(8).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(8)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(8) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =8 \\\\ {x}^{4}-2{x}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-4)({x}^{2}+2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\ge 1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(8)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-2{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-4x \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4({2}^{3})-4(2) \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295820 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295821 13194139534566 true] [17592186295820 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534566 true] [17592186295820 1041 "field-1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295820 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295821 13194139534566 true] [17592186295820 1110 #uuid "2b00cba8-1263-5ce0-b5e1-148424743a6a" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295821 1034 "\\frac{1}{24}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295821 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295821 1111 #uuid "c1f55afc-34aa-53e1-8394-f61ac6801b80" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295822 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295823 13194139534566 true] [17592186295822 1089 "q-342562" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295822 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534566 true] [17592186295822 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/08ce8625dcad742776846497c4e49b5686a893be85ff1bd5426df8f8b9ce882b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on both $y=f(x)$ and $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295822 1113 #uuid "422cbd5e-38e5-544a-b056-53061bf5421a" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295822 1145 "Since $A\\,(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295823 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295824 13194139534566 true] [17592186295823 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534566 true] [17592186295823 1041 "field-1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295823 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295824 13194139534566 true] [17592186295823 1110 #uuid "83e16358-519e-5455-b910-148e3f8eb26b" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295824 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295824 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295824 1111 #uuid "77556d4a-1589-5b09-bec1-8ce736a069a3" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295825 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295826 13194139534566 true] [17592186295825 1089 "q-342570" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295825 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534566 true] [17592186295825 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/feb02396a635c3f416b07383b03269e12fde738da3345aa6d6d9fe5350745746.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,0.5)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(0.5,1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4},$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295825 1113 #uuid "3d0c5039-62c4-5869-afca-b3d561b286f4" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295825 1145 "Since $B\\,(0.5,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0.5)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0.5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295826 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295827 13194139534566 true] [17592186295826 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534566 true] [17592186295826 1041 "field-1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295826 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295827 13194139534566 true] [17592186295826 1110 #uuid "f8d23cac-45b4-5c6c-8c7d-2aa0b595e105" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295827 1034 "\\ln(4)" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295827 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295827 1111 #uuid "95051f6c-2cda-512b-a166-85bf75a4fb6d" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295828 1034 "1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295828 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295828 1111 #uuid "9ac894c4-80f6-5d55-8c9a-7c86cba29ce3" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295829 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295830 13194139534566 true] [17592186295829 1089 "q-342582" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295829 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534566 true] [17592186295829 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and the point $P(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295829 1113 #uuid "c8afa493-ae6a-5bc8-a39b-9bb74c7ad881" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295829 1145 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295830 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295831 13194139534566 true] [17592186295830 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534566 true] [17592186295830 1041 "field-1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295830 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295831 13194139534566 true] [17592186295830 1110 #uuid "16ce9f09-7db6-5f63-94a1-ce00fb0d1697" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295831 1034 "\\frac{1}{8}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295831 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295831 1111 #uuid "635ae1bd-f5f8-56de-b9e5-7ff6a429bfb2" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295832 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295833 13194139534566 true] [17592186295832 1089 "q-342583" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295832 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534566 true] [17592186295832 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295832 1113 #uuid "693dd3d0-3454-5894-a36e-0df2728d3681" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295832 1145 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295833 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295834 13194139534566 true] [17592186295833 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534566 true] [17592186295833 1041 "field-1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295833 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295834 13194139534566 true] [17592186295833 1110 #uuid "4aee9e45-6561-505c-b53a-1631b71fe964" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295834 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295834 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295834 1111 #uuid "205c7a5e-3ebd-5d2e-8080-e01818835b75" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295835 1034 "6" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295835 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295835 1111 #uuid "23122002-68de-55a3-8698-55d189a802c6" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295836 1017 #edb/ref 17592186295837 13194139534566 true] [17592186295836 1089 "q-342599" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295836 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534566 true] [17592186295836 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-x.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain $x\\le -1,$ determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295836 1113 #uuid "08e8f5ed-5b1e-519e-b4c4-4b136ca13f18" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295836 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x & =2 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain of $y=f(x)$ is $x\\le -1,$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(-1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}-x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295837 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295838 13194139534566 true] [17592186295837 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295839 13194139534566 true] [17592186295837 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534566 true] [17592186295837 1041 "field-1" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295837 1104 #edb/ref 17592186295838 13194139534566 true] [17592186295837 1110 #uuid "03616ae6-f9da-5572-920b-0d866b07f62d" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295838 1034 "-\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295838 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295838 1111 #uuid "0f224817-f1c2-52b8-a838-c6627808bcbe" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295839 1034 "3" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295839 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534566 true] [17592186295839 1111 #uuid "571a121e-249d-519e-92f9-1f1502bde7c8" 13194139534566 true] [17592186295840 1000 #edb/ref 17592186295841 1319